    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "but that's the thing, it shouldn't have to be \"perfect\" to fool the general public. most people just see a celebrity and move on, they aren't looking for frame-by-frame glitches. if they used an AI-driven overlay or some kind of real-time deepfake tech during live interviews, it would explain exactly why the movements feel so uncanny and \"laggy\" like I said. it's not about being perfect, it's about being good enough for a 4k screen. if you look at how much tech has changed since that london incident, doesn't it make more sense that they'd use a high-end digital proxy to protect the \"brand\" rather than risk the real guy? it would explain why his personality seems so... hollowed out lately.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
